<header class="ed-hero bg-inverse pb-14 pt-32 text-inverse-ink md:pt-40"><div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6"><p class="mb-4 text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-accent">The Naked Tech field guide</p><h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight md:text-5xl">How to set up a simple backup for one home computer</h1><p class="ed-deck">Choose what matters, select a destination, establish a routine and prove that at least one file can be restored.</p></div></header>
<article class="ed-guide bg-canvas py-12 md:py-16" aria-label="How to set up a simple backup for one home computer"><div class="ed-layout">
{{ contents([{"id": "start", "title": "Find your starting point"}, {"id": "scope-and-health", "title": "Check the computer and define the scope"}, {"id": "external-storage", "title": "Choose a destination and configure a routine"}, {"id": "cloud-storage", "title": "Confirm what is actually included"}, {"id": "sample-check", "title": "Prove one safe restore"}, {"id": "related", "title": "What to read next"}]) }}
<div class="ed-reading guide-reading text-lg">
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">A backup is a separate copy of files that you can use after loss or damage. Sync keeps locations aligned; a clone copies a drive or system. Restoring means retrieving a saved copy, while recovering missing or damaged data may need a different assessment. This guide covers a bounded starting point: one working personal computer, one user and one backup destination. One destination is a useful start, but it cannot protect against every event. Important files may warrant additional independent copies and an off-site copy; plan that separately rather than assuming this first routine is complete protection.</p>
<section class="ed-start" id="start" aria-labelledby="start-heading"><p class="ed-label">Start here</p><h2 id="start-heading">Start with a safe scope</h2><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Begin with files you could not easily recreate: documents, photographs, records and project folders. Do not assume that every folder, application, setting or online account is included. Check the selected folders and the destination before relying on the routine.</p><div class="ed-choices">
{{ choice("External storage", "Compare a disconnected external drive with a cloud destination, including capacity, routine and access limits.", "#external-storage", "startup", "/img/guides/backup-essentials-v2.webp", "An external drive beside a folder and document.", "Read this part") }}
{{ choice("Cloud storage", "Understand internet access, account control, synchronisation and version-retention questions before choosing cloud storage.", "#cloud-storage", "applications", "/img/guides/backup-essentials-v2.webp", "A dark cloud and document above a laptop.", "Read this part") }}
{{ choice("Sample check", "Use one disposable text file to test whether the backup completes and a separate copy can be restored.", "#sample-check", "online", "/img/guides/backup-essentials-v2.webp", "Two separate documents examined with a magnifying glass.", "Read this part") }}
</div></section>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="scope-and-health"><span aria-hidden="true">01</span>Check the computer and define the scope</h2>
{% call note("care", "Take care", "Check the source before making changes") %}<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Before setting anything up, pause if the drive is failing, files are missing or unreadable, or malware may be present. Continued writes can complicate assessment. Deal with the immediate risk first.</p>{% endcall %}<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Make a short list of the folders that matter and note whether they are local, synchronised or stored only online. A visible filename does not prove that a usable local copy exists. For preparation around a Windows move, see <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="/guides/moving-to-a-new-windows-computer/">moving to a new Windows computer</a> and the <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="/guides/new-windows-computer-move-checklist/">new Windows computer move checklist</a>; those guides do not replace a backup.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Keep recovery codes and credentials out of notes, screenshots and backup-test files.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="external-storage"><span aria-hidden="true">02</span>Choose a destination and configure a routine</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">An external drive can be convenient when you have enough capacity and can connect it regularly. Disconnecting it between backups limits some risks, but it also means the routine stops when the drive is unavailable or forgotten. Label the destination and decide where it will be stored.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">For Windows, <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/experience/backup-recovery/backup-and-restore-with-file-history">File History</a> can use selected libraries or folders on an external drive or network location. For a Mac, <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.apple.com/en-au/104984">Time Machine</a> uses a local backup destination. Adding a destination may offer to erase it; stop unless it is deliberately empty or its contents are verified elsewhere.</p><h3 class="mb-3 mt-8 text-xl font-bold">Find the controls for your computer</h3>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">On Windows, open Control Panel → System and Security → Save backup copies of your files with File History. Inspect the selected drive and included folders before turning it on. <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/experience/backup-recovery/backup-and-restore-with-file-history">Microsoft explains File History setup</a>.</p>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">On macOS Ventura 13 or later, open System Settings → General → Time Machine → Add Backup Disk. Follow <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.apple.com/en-au/104984">Apple's instructions for your macOS version</a>. Inspect the destination before proceeding. If asked to erase a drive containing anything you need, cancel. Store any backup-encryption password somewhere you control; you will need it to restore. Check progress and the latest completed backup in the Time Machine menu.</p>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Cloud storage may be easier to reach when the computer and internet are available, but it depends on account access, provider behaviour, storage limits and a clear distinction between backup and sync. Compare both options in <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="/guides/external-drive-or-cloud-backup/">external drive or cloud: choosing a computer backup destination</a>.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="cloud-storage"><span aria-hidden="true">03</span>Confirm what is actually included</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">After configuration, inspect the selected folders, destination and recent-backup status. A routine should answer: what is copied, where it goes, how often it runs, how long versions remain and what happens when the destination or internet is unavailable.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Windows Backup can use a personal Microsoft account and selected folders through OneDrive, but it is not a disk clone or a promise that every application is restored. <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/experience/backup-recovery/back-up-and-restore-with-windows-backup">Review the current Windows Backup description</a> before treating it as part of your plan.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Do not infer protection from a green-looking sync icon. With OneDrive Files On-Demand, an online-only file needs internet access, and a synchronised deletion can propagate. <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/onedrive/save-disk-space-with-onedrive-files-on-demand-for-windows">Microsoft explains online-only files and deletion behaviour</a>.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="sample-check"><span aria-hidden="true">04</span>Prove one safe restore</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Create a disposable plain-text file containing no personal data. Put a fictional sample label, date and known sentence in it, then save it inside a folder that the selected backup includes and that is locally available. Wait for a verified completed backup containing the sample.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Keep the original and restore a separate copy with a different path or name. Never delete or overwrite an important file. If the restore route is unclear, stop. Follow <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="/guides/test-a-backup-file-restore/">the bounded file-restore test</a>, which covers the different Windows and Mac behaviours.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Record the date, source, version or backup point, destination and outcome. One successful sample demonstrates a route worth repeating; it does not prove exhaustive protection.</p>
